16. Optimum Currency Area
Paper: International Economics (ECNCC301) | Unit: III | Topic: Economic integration – Optimum currency area
Introduction
An optimum currency area (OCA) is a region in which sharing a single currency (or irrevocably fixed exchange rates) maximises economic efficiency. The theory was pioneered by Robert Mundell (1961), and developed by McKinnon (1963) and Kenen (1969).
Costs and benefits of a common currency
Benefits: lower transaction costs, no exchange rate uncertainty, price transparency, more trade and investment, credibility for monetary policy.
Costs: loss of independent monetary policy and the exchange rate as a shock absorber.
Criteria for an OCA
1. Labour mobility (Mundell): if shocks hit one region, workers can move from it to areas of employment, replacing exchange rate adjustment.
2. Wage and price flexibility across regions.
3. Openness (McKinnon): in very open economies, exchange rate changes quickly pass into prices, so they are less useful, making a common currency less costly.
4. Product diversification (Kenen): diversified economies suffer fewer asymmetric shocks.
5. Fiscal integration: a central budget transfers funds to hit regions.
6. Similarity of inflation rates and business cycles (symmetry of shocks).
7. Political willingness for integration.
Endogeneity of OCA
Frankel and Rose argued that a currency union itself raises trade integration and business cycle correlation, so a region may satisfy the criteria after joining, not before.
The euro area as a test case
The euro has delivered lower transaction costs and trade gains, but the Eurozone crisis (2010-12) exposed weaknesses: limited labour mobility, no common fiscal transfer system, and asymmetric shocks. Greece, Spain and Ireland could not devalue or use national monetary policy.
Relevance for India and South Asia
South Asian economies have low intra-regional trade, diverse structures and weak political integration, so a common currency is not feasible in the near term.
Conclusion
OCA theory is a cost-benefit framework: the more a region meets the criteria, the greater the net gain from a single currency.